# Intuitive Navigation & Visual Hierarchy — Implementation Playbook

_A compact, tool‑agnostic standard for AI agents building slick frontends._  
**Scope:** Navigation, Information Architecture (IA), wayfinding, and visual hierarchy **only** (no feature‑specific flows).  
**Targets:** Desktop & mobile web apps. Minimum WCAG 2.1 AA intent for readability and affordances.

---

## 1) Information Architecture (IA) — before pixels

**Goal:** Users find what they seek in ≤3 clicks/taps without cognitive strain.

- **Card‑sorting¹**: Group features/content by user intent, not org chart. Prefer “Jobs to be done” categories over internal module names.
- **Mutual exclusivity / collective exhaustiveness (MECE):** Top‑level nav items don’t overlap; together they cover the product.
- **Depth limit:** 1 global nav → 1 local nav (tabs/sections) → content pane. Avoid 3+ stacked nav tiers.
- **Name things by outcome:** _Dashboard, Projects, Activity, Settings_ > _Home, Modules, Utilities_.
- **Sitemaps:** Keep a single source of truth. Each page has exactly one canonical path.
- **Search as a first‑class citizen** where content volume is high. Don’t make search the only path.

> **Litmus:** A new user can sketch your sitemap from memory after a 60‑second tour.

---

## 2) Global Navigation — clarity, not cleverness

**Goal:** Obvious wayfinding and instant sense of place.

- **Persistent global nav:** Top bar or left rail (do not hide on scroll). Always visible “You are here” indicator.
- **5±2 rule:** 3–7 primary nav items. Collapse the rest under **More** with typed search inside.
- **Predictable placement:** Brand left, primary nav center/left, account/help right. Mobile: hamburger → sheet.
- **Labels over icons:** Use words. Icons may accompany, not replace, labels (except universally recognized ones: ⚙, 🔍, ❓).
- **Current location states:** Active item = bold + high‑contrast pill/underline + ARIA `aria-current="page"`.
- **Breadcrumbs** for any depth >1: _Section / Subsection / Current_. Each crumb is a link except the last.
- **Escape hatches:** Global search, Home, and “Back to list” persistently available.

**Anti‑patterns to avoid**

- Hiding primary navigation behind tooltips, progressive hovers, or mystery‑meat icons.
- Re‑labeling the same page differently across contexts.
- Auto‑collapsing nav without an explicit user action.

---

## 3) Local Navigation — tabs, sub‑sections, and lists

**Goal:** Make neighbors and boundaries obvious inside a section.

- **Tabs for peers, accordions for depth.** Tabs switch datasets; accordions reveal details.
- **Left align tabs; prevent overflow** with scrollable “ink bar” and visible first/last affordances.
- **Keep tab count ≤6.** If more, replace with segmented control or sub‑menu.
- **Sticky sub‑nav on scroll** so users retain orientation in long documents.
- **List/Detail pattern:** Lists own the left; detail owns the right (desktop). Mobile uses master→detail push with “Back to list”.
- **Collection empty‑states** explain what belongs here + CTA to create/import. Don’t show empty tables.

---

## 4) Visual Hierarchy — make scanning effortless

**Goal:** Users extract the gist in 3 seconds; details on demand.

### 4.1 Typographic scale (baseline)

- **Base size:** 16px body (1.0–1.5rem fluid), 1.5 line‑height.
- **Scale:** `H1 32–40`, `H2 24–28`, `H3 20–22`, `H4 18`, `Body 16`, `Caption 12–14`.
- **Weight strategy:** Use weight, not color, for emphasis (e.g., 600/700 for headings, 500 for labels).
- **One font family** for UI copy; max two with restraint (UI + numeric/mono).

### 4.2 Spacing rhythm

- **8‑pt system:** Space in multiples of 4/8 (`4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32…`). Consistency > perfection.
- **Section padding:** `24–32` desktop, `16–24` mobile.
- **Density switch:** Offer `comfortable | compact` modes; never reduce line-height below 1.3 in compact.

### 4.3 Color & emphasis

- **Contrast:** WCAG AA text contrast (≥4.5:1); interactive states meet ≥3:1.
- **Color is secondary:** Use color to _reinforce_, not to encode alone; pair with icons/labels.
- **Focus & selection:** Highly visible focus rings; selected row/card has a background elevation, not just a border.

### 4.4 Layout & flow

- **F‑ and Z‑patterns:** Key content at left/top; primary action aligned with the reading flow.
- **One primary action per view** (prominent button); others demoted to secondary/tertiary.
- **Chunking:** Group related controls into cards/sections with clear headings and 2–5 items each.
- **Above‑the‑fold contract:** Users must see page purpose + primary CTA without scrolling on typical laptop widths.

---

## 5) Wayfinding & State — “where am I, what can I do?”

**Goal:** Continuous orientation and system status visibility.

- **Page titles match nav labels** and `<title>`; first H1 equals the nav item.
- **Persistent context bars** in complex areas (filters active, scope, count of results).
- **Loading skeletons** (not spinners) for lists/cards; replace in ≤300ms where possible.
- **Empty, loading, error, and success** states are distinct, instructive, and reversible where safe.
- **Inline validation** on forms with actionable messages (“Use at least 8 characters”) — never modal walls of errors.
- **Undo > confirm:** Prefer reversible actions + toast with _Undo_ over modal confirmations.

---

## 6) Navigation Patterns — choose deliberately

**Pick one primary pattern and stick to it across the app.**

1. **Top bar + sticky sub‑nav** (content‑heavy sites).
2. **Left rail + content pane** (tools, dashboards, CRUD).
3. **Card index → detail drill‑in** (collections/marketplaces).

**Do not mix patterns** within sibling sections. Consistency reduces mental parsing cost.

---

## 7) Scannability Heuristics — for short attention spans

**Goal:** Make every page “glance‑able.”

- **Rule of 3s:** Max three key numbers, three primary blocks, three prominent CTAs per page.
- **First sentence first:** Put the most important info in the first line of a paragraph/card.
- **Icon + label pairs:** Small, meaningful icons accelerate scanning; never icon‑only.
- **Table affordances:** First column is the entity; right edge holds actions. Sticky header + first column.
- **Progressive disclosure:** Default collapsed details; expand on interaction. Avoid always‑expanded advanced settings.
- **Micro‑copy:** 6–12 words max per helper hint. Replace jargon with plain language.

---

## 8) Mobile Navigation — parity without pain

**Goal:** Same mental model; optimized interactions.

- **Single‑column flow**; collapsible sections; sticky primary CTA at bottom when relevant.
- **Hamburger → full‑height sheet** with search at top; active item highlighted.
- **Bottom “utility bar”** for most‑used actions (max 5). Label each icon.
- **Touch targets ≥40×40px**; min 12px gaps; no nested hover menus.
- **Back to list** persists on detail views; don’t rely on browser back only.

---

## 9) Copy & Labeling — the cheapest UX win

**Goal:** Users never guess what a thing means.

- **Nouns for places, verbs for actions.** _Projects, Activity, Settings_ vs. _Projectize_.
- **Avoid internal jargon.** If a term is domain‑specific, add a tooltip definition.
- **Consistent casing:** Title case for nav, sentence case for labels helps scan patterns.
- **CTA verbs are concrete:** _Create project_, _Invite member_, _Export CSV_. No “Proceed”/“OK”.

---

## 10) Measurement & Guardrails — keep yourself honest

**Ship with checks; do not rely on vibes.**

- **Findability test:** 5 users, 5 tasks, target success ≥90% in ≤3 clicks/taps each.
- **Nav comprehension:** Tree‑testing with 20 participants; mis‑routes <15%.
- **Scroll depth:** ≥80% of users see the primary CTA within 5s on a cold load.
- **Time‑to‑gist:** New users can state page purpose in ≤10s (interview prompt).
- **Heatmaps:** Nav items with <5% clicks after 30 days get merged/retired.
- **A11y spot‑check:** Keyboard‑only traversal of a full task succeeds end‑to‑end.

---

## 11) Do/Don’t Gallery (quick reference)

**Do**

- Keep primary nav visible and labeled.
- Use one hierarchy of emphasis: size → weight → color → position.
- Surface page purpose + primary action above the fold.
- Provide breadcrumbs for depth and search for breadth.

**Don’t**

- Hide nav behind hover reveals or icon‑only toolbars.
- Use multiple conflicting button styles on one page.
- Over‑segment content into too many cards with single items.
- Change labels for the same destination in different contexts.

---

## 13) Pre‑Ship Checklist (copy/paste)

- [ ] Global nav visible, 3–7 items, labeled; active state clear.
- [ ] page titles match nav and H1.
- [ ] Primary CTA visible above the fold at common widths (1280×800).
- [ ] Tab/section structure consistent; ≤6 tabs; sticky sub‑nav on scroll.
- [ ] Typographic scale and 8‑pt spacing applied consistently.
- [ ] Lists have sticky header/first-col; actions aligned right; empty‑states instructive.
- [ ] Keyboard traversal covers nav → content → actions without traps.
- [ ] Mobile: single column, reachable primary CTA, hamburger sheet with search.
- [ ] Copy uses nouns for places, verbs for actions; icons always have labels.

---

### Footnotes

1. Card sorting/tree testing can be lightweight with internal users or a quick remote tool; do it once before design and once after shipping the first IA to validate.
